{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"competitive-programming","title":"Competitive Programming & Fast I/O","group":"Modern C++ & Interview Prep","order":76,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">C++ dominates contests because of speed and the STL. A few habits — fast I/O, <code>long long</code>, the right container — prevent most wrong answers and time-limit errors.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-template\"></i>The template</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++ — contest template (GCC)</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;          // GCC-only “everything” header; clang/MSVC: include what you use\nusing namespace std;\nusing ll = long long;\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);   // don't sync C++ streams with C stdio\n    cin.tie(nullptr);              // don't flush cout before every cin\n\n    int t;\n    cin &gt;&gt; t;\n    while (t--) {\n        int n;\n        cin &gt;&gt; n;\n        vector&lt;ll&gt; a(n);\n        for (auto&amp; x : a) cin &gt;&gt; x;\n        ll best = *max_element(a.begin(), a.end());\n        ll sum = accumulate(a.begin(), a.end(), 0LL);\n        cout &lt;&lt; best &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sum &lt;&lt; '\\n';     // '\\n', never endl in hot loops\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++ — the same, portable</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;numeric&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nusing ll = long long;\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n    int t;\n    cin &gt;&gt; t;\n    while (t--) {\n        int n;\n        cin &gt;&gt; n;\n        vector&lt;ll&gt; a(n);\n        for (auto&amp; x : a) cin &gt;&gt; x;\n        cout &lt;&lt; *max_element(a.begin(), a.end()) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; accumulate(a.begin(), a.end(), 0LL) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5 8\n1000000000 2000000000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Portability</div><p><code>&lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;</code> exists only in GCC's libstdc++ (the usual contest compiler); it doesn't exist with clang/libc++ on macOS. Include the real headers when you're not on a judge.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-checklist\"></i>Habits that save you</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Trap</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>int</code> overflow (sums, products &gt; 2·10⁹)</td><td><code>long long</code> (<code>ll</code>) everywhere sums live; <code>1LL * a * b</code></td></tr><tr><td>Slow I/O with 10⁶ numbers</td><td><code>sync_with_stdio(false)</code> + <code>cin.tie(nullptr)</code>, <code>'\\n'</code> instead of <code>endl</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>unordered_map</code> hacked with collisions</td><td>Custom hash with a random seed, or <code>map</code></td></tr><tr><td>Recursion depth 10⁵–10⁶</td><td>Iterative DFS with an explicit stack</td></tr><tr><td>Large local arrays → stack overflow</td><td>Global arrays or <code>vector</code></td></tr><tr><td>Comparing doubles</td><td>Use an epsilon, or stay in integers</td></tr><tr><td><code>v.size() - 1</code> when empty</td><td>Cast: <code>(int)v.size() - 1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Modular arithmetic</td><td><code>(a % M + M) % M</code> for negatives; multiply in <code>long long</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Handy snippets</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;bit&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;numeric&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing ll = long long;\nconstexpr ll MOD = 1'000'000'007;\n\nll power(ll base, ll exp, ll mod) {                  // fast modular exponentiation\n    ll result = 1;\n    base %= mod;\n    while (exp &gt; 0) {\n        if (exp &amp; 1) result = result * base % mod;\n        base = base * base % mod;\n        exp &gt;&gt;= 1;\n    }\n    return result;\n}\n\nstd::vector&lt;int&gt; sieve(int n) {                      // primes up to n\n    std::vector&lt;bool&gt; composite(n + 1);\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; primes;\n    for (int i = 2; i &lt;= n; ++i) {\n        if (composite[i]) continue;\n        primes.push_back(i);\n        for (ll j = 1LL * i * i; j &lt;= n; j += i) composite[j] = true;\n    }\n    return primes;\n}\n\nstruct DSU {                                         // union–find\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; parent, size;\n    explicit DSU(int n) : parent(n), size(n, 1) { std::iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0); }\n    int find(int x) { return parent[x] == x ? x : parent[x] = find(parent[x]); }\n    bool unite(int a, int b) {\n        a = find(a); b = find(b);\n        if (a == b) return false;\n        if (size[a] &lt; size[b]) std::swap(a, b);\n        parent[b] = a; size[a] += size[b];\n        return true;\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; power(2, 1'000'000, MOD) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    auto p = sieve(30);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; p.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; p.back() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    DSU d(5);\n    d.unite(0, 1); d.unite(3, 4); d.unite(1, 4);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (d.find(0) == d.find(3)) &lt;&lt; (d.find(2) == d.find(0)) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; __builtin_popcount(13) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; __builtin_ctz(8) &lt;&lt; ' '\n              &lt;&lt; std::popcount(13u) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::bit_width(1000u) - 1 &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // portable &lt;bit&gt; versions\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>235042059\n10 29\n10\n3 3 3 9</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>GCC/Clang builtins: <code>__builtin_popcount(x)</code> (set bits), <code>__builtin_ctz(x)</code> (trailing zeros), <code>__builtin_clz(x)</code> (leading zeros); libstdc++ also has <code>std::__lg(x)</code> (floor log2). Portable C++20 equivalents live in <code>&lt;bit&gt;</code>: <code>std::popcount</code>, <code>std::countr_zero</code>, <code>std::bit_width(x) - 1</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-gauge\"></i>Operations per second</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>n</th><th>Feasible complexity (≈10⁸ simple ops/s)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>≤ 10–12</td><td>O(n!)</td></tr><tr><td>≤ 20–25</td><td>O(2ⁿ)</td></tr><tr><td>≤ 500</td><td>O(n³)</td></tr><tr><td>≤ 5 000</td><td>O(n²)</td></tr><tr><td>≤ 10⁶</td><td>O(n log n)</td></tr><tr><td>≤ 10⁸</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>&gt; 10⁹</td><td>O(log n) or O(1) — math</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What do ios::sync_with_stdio(false) and cin.tie(nullptr) do?</summary><div><p>The first stops C++ streams from synchronising with C's <code>stdio</code> buffers after every operation, letting <code>cin</code>/<code>cout</code> buffer independently (after it, don't mix <code>scanf</code>/<code>printf</code> with <code>cin</code>/<code>cout</code>). The second unties <code>cin</code> from <code>cout</code>, so <code>cout</code> isn't flushed before every read. Together they make stream I/O about as fast as <code>scanf</code>/<code>printf</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}